<main>
    <div class="qt-wrap">
        

        <section class="qt-toolhead">
            <nav class="qt-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span>Amounts and words</span><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span>Lakh Crore in Numbers</span></nav>
            <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3 mt-5">
                <span class="qt-sticker is-amounts">Amounts and words</span>
                <span class="qt-stamp">Updated {{ page.date | humanDate }}</span>
            </div>
            <h1 class="qt-h1 mt-4">Lakh Crore in Numbers</h1>
            <p class="qt-lede mt-4">1 lakh crore = {{ one.crore }} crore = {{ one.indian }} = one trillion</p>
        </section>

        <!-- Converter -->
        <div class="qt-card">
            <label for="lcInput" class="qt-label mb-2">How many lakh crore?</label>
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
                <input type="text" id="lcInput" value="9.8" autocomplete="off" autocapitalize="off" spellcheck="false" maxlength="40"
                    class="qt-input"
                    oninput="convertLakhCrore()" placeholder="9.8">
                <span class="text-lg font-semibold whitespace-nowrap">lakh crore</span>
            </div>
            <div id="lcBox" class="qt-card is-amounts mt-4 md:p-5" aria-live="polite">
                <p id="lcDigits" class="text-xl md:text-3xl font-extrabold tabular break-all tracking-tight">{{ nine8.indian }}</p>
                <p id="lcWords" class="mt-1 text-base md:text-lg">{{ nine8.words }}</p>
                <p id="lcMeta" class="mt-1 text-sm">{{ nine8.crore }} crore · {{ nine8.trillion }} trillion · {{ nine8.digits }} digits, {{ nine8.zeros }} zeros</p>
            </div>
            <p id="lcError" class="hidden mt-3 text-center text-sm font-semibold"></p>
            <p class="text-sm mt-3 text-center">Type the number in front of "lakh crore", for example 270, 9.08 or 1.44. For any other amount use the <a href="/number-to-words/" class="qt-link">Number to Words converter</a>.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Rule -->
        <div class="qt-card mt-8">
            <h2 class="qt-section-title mb-4">The rule that does the work</h2>
            <p class="mb-4">
                A lakh crore is a lakh of crores. One lakh is 1,00,000 and one crore is 1,00,00,000, so one lakh crore is 1 followed by 5 zeros and then 7 more: {{ one.zeros }} zeros in all. That is exactly one trillion.
            </p>
            <p class="mb-4">
                Because the two are equal, you never need to convert. A figure in lakh crore is the same figure in trillion. 9.8 lakh crore is 9.8 trillion and 270 lakh crore is 270 trillion.
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li><strong>To crore:</strong> multiply by 1,00,000. 9.8 lakh crore is {{ nine8.crore }} crore.</li>
                <li><strong>To billion:</strong> multiply by 1,000. 9.8 lakh crore is {{ nine8.billion }} billion.</li>
                <li><strong>To digits:</strong> move the decimal point 12 places to the right. 9.8 becomes {{ nine8.international }}, which in Indian grouping is {{ nine8.indian }}.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <!-- Table -->
        <div class="qt-card mt-8">
            <h2 class="qt-section-title mb-2">Lakh crore table</h2>
            <p class="mb-4">The figures people look up most, plus round numbers. Tap a figure to open it in the full converter with the words and cheque wording.</p>
            <div class="qt-table-wrap">
                <table class="qt-table">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Lakh crore</th>
                            <th>Crore</th>
                            <th>In digits (Indian grouping)</th>
                            <th>Trillion</th>
                            <th>Zeros</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        {% for r in lakhCrore.rows %}
                        <tr>
                            <td class="font-semibold"><a href="/number-to-words/?n={{ r.query }}" class="qt-link">{{ r.value }} lakh crore</a></td>
                            <td class="font-mono">{{ r.crore }}</td>
                            <td class="font-mono whitespace-nowrap">{{ r.indian }}</td>
                            <td>{{ r.trillion }}</td>
                            <td>{{ r.zeros }}</td>
                        </tr>
                        {% endfor %}
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- News -->
        <div class="qt-card mt-8">
            <h2 class="qt-section-title mb-4">Reading a lakh crore figure in the news</h2>
            <p class="mb-4">
                Budget speeches, Reserve Bank reports and Indian newspapers often write large rupee amounts in lakh crore. International reports write the same amounts in trillion. Since one lakh crore is one trillion, the number in front stays the same when you switch between the two.
            </p>
            <p class="mb-4">
                Watch the currency. A headline that says an economy is worth 4 trillion dollars is talking about dollars. To compare it with a rupee figure in lakh crore, first convert dollars to rupees at the exchange rate of that day, then compare. The unit (lakh crore or trillion) and the currency (rupees or dollars) are two separate questions.
            </p>
            <p>
                Watch the decimals too. 9.08 lakh crore and 9.8 lakh crore look close but differ by {{ lakhCrore.gap908 }} crore. Read the figure in crore when the gap matters.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Per person -->
        <div class="qt-card mt-8">
            <h2 class="qt-section-title mb-4">Per person: dividing lakh crore by crore</h2>
            <p class="mb-4">
                A common question is how much a national figure works out to per person, where the population is also in crore. There is a shortcut: lakh crore divided by crore leaves lakh. So divide the two front numbers and read the answer in lakh rupees.
            </p>
            <p>
                Example of the arithmetic only: 205 lakh crore divided among 140 crore people is 205 ÷ 140 = 1.4643 lakh, which is ₹{{ lakhCrore.perPerson }} per person.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Words and bigger -->
        <div class="qt-card mt-8">
            <h2 class="qt-section-title mb-4">Lakh crore in words, and what comes after</h2>
            <p class="mb-4">
                In Indian English, 1 lakh crore is written "One Lakh Crore" and 270 lakh crore is "{{ t270.words }}". In international English the same amounts are one trillion and two hundred seventy trillion.
            </p>
            <p>
                Indian English has no common word above crore, so amounts keep growing in lakh crore. 100 lakh crore ({{ h100.indian }}, {{ h100.zeros }} zeros) is a crore crore, or 100 trillion, but newspapers still write it as 100 lakh crore.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div class="qt-card mt-8">
            <h2 class="qt-section-title mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <div class="space-y-4">
                {% set faqs = [
                    ["How many zeros are in 1 lakh crore?", "1 lakh crore has " ~ one.zeros ~ " zeros. Written out it is " ~ one.indian ~ " in Indian grouping, or " ~ one.international ~ " in the international system."],
                    ["Is 1 lakh crore equal to 1 trillion?", "Yes. 1 lakh crore and 1 trillion are the same number, 10 to the power 12. So any figure in lakh crore is the same figure in trillion: 9.8 lakh crore is 9.8 trillion."],
                    ["How do you write 9.8 lakh crore in numbers?", "9.8 lakh crore is " ~ nine8.indian ~ " in Indian grouping, which is " ~ nine8.crore ~ " crore. In the international system it is " ~ nine8.international ~ ", or 9.8 trillion."],
                    ["How many crore make 1 lakh crore?", "1,00,000 crore make 1 lakh crore, the same way 1,00,000 rupees make 1 lakh. To turn lakh crore into crore, multiply by 1,00,000."],
                    ["How much is 1 lakh crore in billion?", "1 lakh crore is " ~ one.billion ~ " billion. Multiply a lakh crore figure by 1,000 to get billions: 270 lakh crore is " ~ t270.billion ~ " billion."]
                ] %}
                {% for f in faqs %}
                <details class="qt-faq-item">
                    <summary>
                        {{ f[0] }}
                                            </summary>
                    <p class="mt-3 text-sm">{{ f[1] }}</p>
                </details>
                {% endfor %}
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</main>

{% block scripts %}
<script src="/js/number-words.js"></script>
<script>
    function convertLakhCrore() {
        var raw = document.getElementById('lcInput').value.trim();
        // A bare number means lakh crore. If they typed units themselves, read it as written.
        var r = NumberWords.convert(/[a-z]/i.test(raw) ? raw : raw + ' lakh crore');
        var err = document.getElementById('lcError');
        var box = document.getElementById('lcBox');
        if (!raw || r.empty || r.error) {
            err.textContent = raw ? (r.error || '') : '';
            err.classList.toggle('hidden', !raw || !r.error);
            box.classList.add('opacity-40');
            return;
        }
        err.classList.add('hidden');
        box.classList.remove('opacity-40');
        document.getElementById('lcDigits').textContent = r.indianGrouped;
        document.getElementById('lcWords').textContent = r.inWords.replace(/ Rupees( and .* Paise)? Only$/, function (m, p) { return p ? ' Rupees' + p : ''; });
        var parts = NumberWords.inUnit(r.rupees, 7).split('.');
        var crore = NumberWords.groupIndian(BigInt(parts[0])) + (parts[1] ? '.' + parts[1] : '');
        document.getElementById('lcMeta').textContent = crore + ' crore · ' + NumberWords.inUnit(r.rupees, 12) + ' trillion · ' + r.digitCount + ' digits, ' + r.zeroCount + ' zeros';
    }
    window.addEventListener('load', convertLakhCrore);
</script>
{% endblock %}
